'''Kazabune''' ({{nihongo|傘船|Kazabune|lit. umbrella boats}}) was a Sanese exile colony on the southwestern coast of [[Lurandia]], founded around 1338–1340 by remnants of the [[Kurokoma clan]] following their purge in the [[Kurokoma-Kanemoto War]]. One of the earliest sustained Sanese presences in the New World, the colony survived for several generations through fishing, limited trade with indigenous populations, and occasional mercenary work for Fosian colonial ventures, before fading from the historical record in the fifteenth century. Its surviving population was absorbed by the [[Tamagawa clan|Tamagawa]] expedition of 1478, whose settlement — founded on and around the old colony's territory — became the [[Kavik|Confederation of Kavik]], the second Sanese settler state of Lurandia after [[Kaneda]].

==Etymology==
The name Kazabune (傘船) consists of two kanji: 傘 (''kasa''), "umbrella," and 船 (''fune''), "boat." Tradition holds that the name originated during the exile voyage itself, when the Kurokoma leadership — the "black colt" clan (黒駒) of [[Nagashima]] — rigged shades of sailcloth over the decks of their ships while crossing the equatorial latitudes of the [[Shendan Ocean]], a sight their descendants remembered as a fleet of umbrella boats. In the colony's own speech the reading drifted from the standard ''Kasa-fune'' to ''Kazabune'', a phonological shift that survives in the modern [[Kavik Sanese]] "''kaza''" reading of the character 傘.

==History==
===The Kurokoma exile===
{{Main|Umijin|Kurokoma-Kanemoto War}}

The [[Kurokoma clan]] — the dominant power among the southern [[Umijin]] sea lords of [[Nagashima]] — was systematically purged from all positions of shogunal authority by the victorious [[Kanemoto Shogunate]] in 1337. Facing complete political annihilation, the remnants of the Kurokoma leadership and their most loyal retainers chose exile over submission. Around 1338–1340, they embarked on a trans-oceanic voyage across the Shendan Ocean, navigating to the rugged southwestern coast of Lurandia — a remote, temperate region bordering arctic climate — where they established a permanent settlement.

The voyage was the longest sustained migration in pre-modern Sanese history, and it demonstrated the extraordinary maritime capabilities of the exiled Umijin clans: a fleet of cold-water whalers and strait pilots, built and provisioned by a caste the inland court had just declared extinct, crossing an ocean the court insisted no Sanese vessel could survive.

===Colony life===
The exiles landed in the territory of the [[Kavikmiut|Qilalumiut]] nation, whose initial contact with the newcomers was cautious but largely peaceful. The colony survived on a combination of deep-water fishing, limited trade with the indigenous populations, and occasional mercenary work for Fosian colonial ventures probing the Lurandian coast. Around 1365, the colonists founded [[Shirone]] (白根, "white root"), the colony's principal settlement, on one of the few sheltered harbors of the southwestern coast.

Cut off from the archipelago by distance and by the political impossibility of return, Kazabune developed in near-total isolation. Its population — never more than a few thousand — retained the language of the late [[Hiraizumi-Daishin period]], the sea-deity cults of the Umijin, and the clan structure of Nagashima, while absorbing through marriage and adoption a growing proportion of indigenous Kavikmiut and, later, Dagit and Zong settlers drawn by the colony's position on the Fosian trade routes.

===Decline and absorption===
By the mid-fifteenth century, Kazabune had faded from the historical record. The reasons were the quiet ones that dissolve small colonies: a founding population too small to sustain itself, a homeland that could not resupply it, and the slow absorption of its people into the indigenous nations and Fosian trading settlements around it. The colony left no chronicle of its own ending.

When the [[Tamagawa clan|Tamagawa]] expedition — a maritime family of [[Kii Prefecture|Kii]] in [[Mitoyagi]], sailing with Zong merchants of the [[Lim clan|Lim]] and Yanbaruan mariners of the [[Higa clan|Higa]] — made landfall on the same southwestern coast on 28 March 1478, they found the remnants of the Kurokoma colony still living in and around the old settlement of Shirone. The Tamagawa settlement absorbed the surviving Kazabunese population and refounded Shirone on the old site, and the colony's territory became the heartland of what developed into the [[Kavik|Confederation of Kavik]]. Kavik thus superseded Kazabune in the same place: the second Sanese settler state of Lurandia grew directly over the first.

==Legacy==
Kazabune survives into the present as a substrate — demographic, linguistic, and institutional — of the Confederation of Kavik.

* '''Language:''' The archaic Nagashima Sanese of the colony reinforced the conservative features of the [[Tamagawa clan|Tamagawa]] settlers' own dialect in the formation of [[Kavik Sanese]]. Linguists attribute part of Kavik Sanese's archaic phonology and its distinctive whaling and navigation lexicon to the Kazabunese population absorbed in 1478.
* '''Orthography:''' The "''kaza''" reading of the character 傘 — a Kazabunese phonological shift from standard ''kasa'' — is preserved in Kavik Sanese to the present day.
* '''Institutions:''' The name survives in Kavik commercial institutions descended from the colony's trading tradition, most prominently the Shirone financial house [[Shirone Capital]] (傘船資本), which retains the colony's kanji in its own name.
* '''Historiography:''' In Kavik national history, Kazabune functions as the "first settlement" — the proof that the Sanese arrival on the southwestern coast was not a single event but a continuous presence stretching back to the fourteenth century. In Nakamizuan historiography, the colony is the final chapter of the Kurokoma tragedy: the sea lords who built an empire's navy, were purged by it, and replanted its maritime civilization on the far side of the world.

